# USER

I get prompted whether I want to delete "*greger-terminal-nnn*" because it has a running process. Get rid of that

# THINKING

Signature: 

The user is getting prompted to delete buffers like "*greger-terminal-nnn*" because they have running processes. This is happening in the vterm implementation. I need to find where these buffers are being created and make sure they don't prompt for deletion.

Looking at the code, I can see that in the `greger--process-terminal-sequences-with-vterm` function in greger.el, there's a temporary vterm buffer being created:

```elisp
(defun greger--process-terminal-sequences-with-vterm (text)
  "Process terminal control sequences in TEXT using vterm for accurate rendering.

This function creates a temporary vterm buffer, sends the text to it, and
extracts the properly rendered output. This ensures that all ANSI escape
sequences are handled correctly with full terminal emulation."
  (let ((vterm-buffer (generate-new-buffer " *greger-terminal*")))
    (unwind-protect
        (with-current-buffer vterm-buffer
          ;; Set up vterm environment for non-interactive use
          (let ((vterm-kill-buffer-on-exit nil)
                (vterm-shell "cat"))  ; Use cat as shell to just echo input
            (vterm-mode)
            
            ;; Send the text to vterm for processing
            (vterm-send-string text)
            
            ;; Wait a brief moment for vterm to process the text
            (sit-for 0.1)
            
            ;; Extract the rendered content
            (buffer-string)))
      ;; Clean up the temporary buffer
      (kill-buffer vterm-buffer))))
```

The problem is that when `vterm-mode` is initialized, it creates a process, and when we try to kill the buffer, it prompts because there's a running process. I need to set `set-process-query-on-exit-flag` to nil on the process to prevent this prompt.

Also, in the main vterm implementation in greger-stdlib.el, I already set this flag, but I need to make sure it's being set properly.

# ASSISTANT

You're right! The issue is that the temporary vterm buffers created for terminal sequence processing have running processes, and when they're killed, Emacs prompts about deleting buffers with running processes. I need to set the `set-process-query-on-exit-flag` to nil on those processes.

